isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/29f1e589e31f707106c0627282cddb82053ad236.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":8,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following DFA:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/cefb62d9efadc840f70c5502d4299eff165c096e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following is not accepted by above DFA?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 1001100</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 01001110</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 0000010</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 1000110</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n<em>a</em>, <em>b</em> and <em>c</em> accepted by DFA.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":9,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>What is the language accepted by the following PDA? (\u0001 be the end of string and $ is the initial symbol in stack)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/32719714ddc45b4e9e4119bf22d3358155b2d8a7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/698fcf5146ce7f4febf4c9fadf2f959788f6b21c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/4e52a2416351904a0c18b10e98b1cee343c8546b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/b0756b9ea73e7df718df501889b77b2ae4100948.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/87cb34abf6381fdfe54af66c07c11fb7571b5b64.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4b1a97e26fa1b0a1c6c62b0b5bfda879cdf624da.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":10,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider <em>P</em>, <em>Q</em> and <em>R</em> be language over Σ = {0, 1} represented by the regular expression 0* (10*)*, (0 + 1*)* and (1* 0)* 1* respectively. Which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/f7b169bc5e3d65d949f32e4e4cc86dc175225e64.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/93c0cd92929d20125dc0ae4800ad6b293369ab3c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/775bf00c1b01ccc3fd4f10706a041a88e84cd70f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/94d2a72b848438dd5e6ebad829bbb6fbdd43ae0a.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/dc244262fb194ab3719e921b902e2068be4aa067.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":11,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following problems:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/52e86f4813db217ed52f29e6bc2639204c956d1e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The number of problems which are RE not REC is ________.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2","high":"2"},"solution":"<p>2</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/66516f10d38d84bc6230560c52dc343cd89a0636.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":12,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following are context free?</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/1be135205d15b6baf1a78527ecfd0d6acdc8c60e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of the language</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f65793927d45e5c973dfffe8e8c5b1f17aaaf030.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":13,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Pumping lemma can be used to prove given language is regular.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Given a grammar, checking if the grammar is not regular is decidable problem.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : If L is a regular and M is not a regular language then L.M. is necessarily non-regular.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>4</sub> : The number of derivations step for any strings W of length n is grammar is CNF and GNF form is (2n – 1) and (n) respectively.<br />\nWhich of the following statement is correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is correct</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>4</sub> is correct</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is correct</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is correct</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/bc1d5fb8b8ac1232b1bf5d6b6c1ffa42130bfa98.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":14,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider given languages, <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> is CSL, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is CFL and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is unknown language. Then which of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> If <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is non-regular then <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> ∩ <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is non-regular.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> If <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> ∪ <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is Σ* then <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is universal language.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> If <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> – <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> – <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> are non-regular, then <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is non-regular.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/414490ed6e73fb66eb45d3b99fb800259a129429.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":15,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Which of the decision problems are decidable?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Given two deterministic CFG, <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub>, is <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) ≠ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>).</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Given two non-deterministic CFG, <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub>, is <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) ⊂ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>).</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> Given two grammar <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub>, is <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub> have same production.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Given two non-regular grammar <em>G</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>G</em><sub>2</sub>, is <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>1</sub>) ∩ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>G</em><sub>2</sub>) = <img src=\"/media/033581ef17d5549ac5d05e76696de5eb6fabc994.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/4af2c3f88b100c1b70dfe31a652fa2b1d8aef094.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":16,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following DFA:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f1f92220c0147afc446381d51935658b2f82c4f8.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>The number of states in the minimized DFA of the above DFA will be ________.</div></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"3","high":"3"},"solution":"<p>3</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f3a19a7fd5e2da6b7ace2b42197db8b55b5b21cf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":17,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C,D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following grammars are in Chomsky Normal Form?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/7aa64dfe9eaa8b65c2c7dcf402c527a1b4311321.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/2c4842d4f9af2a6eb1ed6705fb1fae4a0554fb0f.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/111bc42c73dbe31de667ef1f47d1a02c3494fcb5.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/594c51ce55c6800472b6dbdd4d59b2352e0e9710.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\nChomsky Normal format is <em>S</em> &rarr; <em>AB</em> or <em>S</em> &rarr; <em>a</em> where <em>S</em>, <em>A</em>, <em>B</em> are variables and &lsquo;<em>a</em>&rsquo; is constant.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":18,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,C,D","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following languages:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/ca690b124da1776dd5f50a902929bd8da534e1be.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>\n<div>Which one of the following statements is/are false?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is context free but not regular.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> ∩ <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> is non-empty language.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> is regular language but not <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> Both <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> are regular language.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/d3d3a89a840de55bbefe7a3d6c6ada1c62b7a03c.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":19,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements for a language:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If every subset of <em>L</em> is decidable, then <em>L</em> must be CFL.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If <em>L</em> is a regular language, then every subset of <em>L</em> is decidable.<br />\nWhich of the following statements is incorrect?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : If every subset of a language is decidable then it is finite and regular. Otherwise power set of infinite language is undecidable.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : If L is a regular language then every subset is not decidable of L is infinite language.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":20,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Identify the function performed by the following Turing Machine</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/71a4555f811e45d640117bbe9199382f19fc6c55.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> Addition of two binary numbers</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> Increment a binary number by 1</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 1’s complement</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 2’s complement</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/0c5d49f6b64e9c8e1cb8cf55b7f7f8a86f127aff.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":21,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>A</em> ≤<sub><em>m</em></sub> <em>B</em> denotes that language <em>A</em> is mapping reducible to language <em>B</em>.<br />\nWhich of the following is/are true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> If <em>A</em> ≤<sub><em>m</em></sub> B and every subset of <em>B</em> is CFL only if <em>A</em> must be finite language.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> If <em>A</em> ≤<sub>m</sub> <em>B</em> and <em>B</em> is decidable, then <em>A</em> is closed under subset operation.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> If <em>A</em> ≤<em><sub>m</sub></em> <em>B</em> and <em>A</em> is decidable, then <em>B</em> must be finite language.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> If <em>A</em> ≤<em><sub>m</sub></em> <em>B</em> and every subset of <em>A</em> are decidable if <em>B</em> is finite language.</div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, d)<br />\n<em>A</em> &le;<em><sub>m</sub> B</em>, power of language <em>B</em> is greater than equal to <em>A</em>.<br />\nSo, if <em>B</em> is decidable then <em>A</em> is decidable.<br />\nIf B is finite and <em>A</em> is also finite and finite languages are closed under infinite subset.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":22,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>A TM M = ({<em>q</em><sub>0</sub>, <em>q</em><sub>1</sub>, ..., <em>q</em><sub>6</sub>}, {0, 1},{0, 1, B}, δ<em>q</em><sub>0</sub>; <em>B</em>, <span><span><span>Φ\\Phi </span></span></span>}. The transition are</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/0c82e964995675523d1793660a418d5d83ba1ccf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>What is the language accepted by TM where <em>q</em><sub>5</sub> is the final state?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 0* 10*</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/305c61570d0e9a9b70a9ea4ec813b87e5d97a859.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> (0 + 1)*</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/1783fddb57eea47b6063725b9d047e6de36352cb.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6003bcd1d600fbb25144b9e613937ebb9f8a0ec7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":23,"type":"MCQ","correct":"C","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> has the following grammar:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/6d9d6afb6b75014a59c7f4506b38569468cdc80f.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following is true about?\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/f414f5a2df3bb3df1c437572d6a3d77b6a3c9fa9.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>3</sub>, <em>L</em><sub>5</sub> are context free but not <em>L</em><sub>4</sub>.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>5</sub> is regular language but <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>4</sub> are non-regular.</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> is empty language, <em>L</em><sub>4</sub> has same string to <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>5</sub> is CSL.</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/2daa40e3398f49d7154402b317d12d2402242197.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":24,"type":"MCQ","correct":"B","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following Turing Machine:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/a5b8f376dd7e0a299a3ec1c6291b763fbbab12b6.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>If the above TM accepts a language <img src=\"/media/e45a0ccc14451dadb1a235fc6f2a79e41eb84e95.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> then what are the missing transitions at <em>P </em>and <em>Q</em> respectively</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> (<em>Y</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>R</em>) and (<em>X</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>R</em>)</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> (<em>X</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>R</em>) and (<em>Y</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>R</em>)</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> (<em>X</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>L</em>) and (<em>Y</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>R</em>)</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> (<em>Y</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>R</em>) and (<em>X</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>L</em>)</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/aabe1561ff2ca1324ee9346f11d218de7fde6e79.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":25,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Let <em>M</em> = (k, Σ, Γ, Δ, <em>F</em>) be a push down automation, where</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b491e32f70bc48abe2da1560f2ba4c08a2e06773.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following strings is not a member of <em>L</em>(<em>M</em>)?</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>aababb</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>aabab</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>bbaab</em></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>bbabbb</em></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n </p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":26,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":1,"wrong":0.33,"qtext":"<p>Let <img src=\"/media/113f934a1b583c09c9feb1cea167f56da984e41d.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is, where ‘/’ is quotient, ‘–’ is difference and <img src=\"/media/7f1e3ebaf2e13d59d2078b523b6dfba5b4636bfd.png\" align=\"absmiddle\" loading=\"lazy\" /> is complement.</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> {ε}</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/8d6cbcbcabe312364f2f2032f1446af57c2ab303.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/5fbcd9538f1f5929be3bbdfba3cffbdce1014fc0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> None of these</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/cf68669ee7c1b1e8b62eb50aaeab03ecbbcba052.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":27,"type":"MSQ","correct":"B,C,D","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Which of the following language(s) is/are regular?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9675145afcde041cd4a6dbbc7674d65bbc1db12b.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/b984243a305a2b75f813e09403d82855b96e5b50.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/8368e1bdcec3c64986b192403db19ebe610f29e0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/456bd6ab9d3b19c960ebe787be35bdc3996ccab0.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(b, c, d)<br />\n(a) Not regular<br />\n(b) String starting and ending with same symbol, regular language<br />\n(c) Finite language regular<br />\n(d) <em>L</em> = <em>a</em>*, regular language</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":28,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":1,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : All regular languages are linear.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : All linear grammar are regular.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Some DCFL’s are regular.<br />\nWhich of the following statement(s) is correct?</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"2","high":"2"},"solution":"<p>2<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : True<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : False<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : True</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":29,"type":"NAT","correct":"","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Let Σ = {<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>} be an input alphabets where each alphabet is assigned a numeric values a = 3,<br />\n<em>b</em> = 2, <em>c</em> = 1.<br />\nWhat is the number of final states in a minimal DFA that accepts all strings whose product of alphabets is not divisible by 7.</p>\n<p>(empty string does’nt belong to language)</p></div>\n<div><span>NAT</span>","options":[],"nat":{"low":"6","high":"6"},"solution":"<p>6<br />\nPossible number of remainder from a, b and c are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6.<br />\nHence remainder 0 not there is solution, therefore only 6 final states possible in minimal state<br />\nfinite automata.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":30,"type":"MCQ","correct":"A","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following λ-NFA:</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/fae87ada6431802f377443ab5b2fe0b3fc9f870e.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Find the cardinality of epsilon closure of state ‘<em>S</em>’ [λ-closure (q) is the set which contain all the states those are reachable from state <em>q</em> without reading any input symbol]</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> 4</div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> 5</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> 6</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> 7</div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b7f6e455b4be2f9b11a826d5e604b26dc85be3e7.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div> </div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":31,"type":"MSQ","correct":"A,B,C","right":2,"wrong":0,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following non-deterministic finite automation where <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is the start state and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is the final state, given Σ = {0, 1}.</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/273b660f3d89289cbca2be40ec730902b32b2aa2.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>\n<div>Which of the following automata having same behaviour to given automata.</div>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/3420375148801fc54bbafd5bf21762d029eddacf.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9f3f21bf46e029d1ad9cc733db20cfcffacadf34.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/9a4d65149cd5467e4ee708ae5306d12475be5e58.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":true},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <img src=\"/media/03f643bfb9c17601a57612b159a45bf37c249a31.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","isCorrect":false}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)<br />\n(a) NFA without &epsilon;-moves.<br />\n(b) Equivalent to given NFA.<br />\n(c) Equivalent to given NFA.<br />\n(d) NFA without &epsilon;-moves, with same extra connections.</p>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":32,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p><em>L</em><sub>1</sub> : Languages that accept strings over Σ = {a, b} such that length of string is greater than 1 but multiple of 3.<br />\n<em>L</em><sub>2</sub> : Languages such that every string contains 2 a’s and atmost 2 b’s.<br />\n<em>L</em><sub>3</sub> : Correspond to regular expression <em>R</em> where <em>R</em> =10 + 0 + 110* 1 over Σ = {0, 1}.<br />\nLet the number of states in the minimal DFA of <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> be <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>, then which of the following is true?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> S<sub>1</sub> = S<sub>2</sub> < S<sub>3</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> = <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> < <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> < <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> < <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> < <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> < <em>S</em><sub>2</sub></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n<div> </div>\n<div><img src=\"/media/b91c3568c0db791cd1274250989b63abceade4a1.png\" loading=\"lazy\" /></div>","subject":"Technical"},{"qnum":33,"type":"MCQ","correct":"D","right":2,"wrong":0.66,"qtext":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Given a non RE language is uncountable.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Set of all RE languages are countable.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : Given a non-regular language is infinite.<br />\nWhich of the following statements are decidable?</p>","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"B","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"C","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> only</div>","isCorrect":false},{"letter":"D","text":"<div> <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>3</sub></div>","isCorrect":true}],"nat":{"low":"","high":""},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Decidable, as every non RE language is countable.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Decidable<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>3</sub> : All finite language are regular trivial properly hence all non-regular language are infinitedecidable.</p>","subject":"Technical"}],"questionCount":33,"parsedAt":"2026-11-10T20:11:32.184Z"}